Turn per-region selection back off by default, and chain block alignment

Per-region selection wrecked a real stack. On the 993-frame session it was
shipped on by default, and the result was a mosaic of squares with visible
seams and a ghosted Moon.

Cause: the feature ranks frames per tile using the sharpness score, and on this
session that score varies by only ~1.6% between frames. It is measuring sensor
noise, not sharpness (a point established earlier and then not applied when the
feature was made default). Each tile therefore draws a different random subset
of frames, and the seams show.

Fix: `tile_select` defaults to off. A one-time migration clears a stored `true`,
so anyone who ran the broken default is not left with it. The feature stays
available for when the score can tell grain from detail.

Also fixes a real fragility in block merging while I was in there: every block
was aligned directly against the first one. On this session the Moon travels
~1800 px vertically, so by the last blocks the two images barely overlap and
phase correlation picks a wrong peak. Blocks are now aligned in a chain
(block to previous block, shifts accumulated and clamped), which keeps each
step small and reliable.

Verified: same cache, same settings, `tile_select=false` gives a clean, sharp
Moon; `tile_select=true` gives the mosaic.
